{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And why should we die, etc., for the silver is gone, etc.</b> The meaning is that he was not willing to accept payment for the grain in anything but silver, and no other form of payment. For this reason he gathered all the silver that could be found, and now they came with the argument, saying ״why״ etc., for the reason that the silver was exhausted — since they could have paid for the grain in silver's equivalent value. Therefore Joseph answered them that he was willing to accept their livestock in exchange for bread, since the silver was gone. And he was precise in his words, saying <b>if the silver is gone</b> — and without this condition he would not give to them, as long as they still had silver to pay with. And his reason for impoverishing Egypt was so that his brothers would not be poor in the presence of the rich; therefore he devised the plan that they should buy from him with silver. For as long as the livestock was in their hands, their provisions were upon them, and they would need to purchase bread for themselves as well as provisions for the livestock — for presumably there was no herbage and no grass, and they needed to give barley as fodder for the animals. For this reason he left the needs of the livestock upon them and measured out to them in exchange for silver. Through this, the first year was not completed until they were drained and made like an empty vessel devoid of everything. And when his brothers came, they were rich in the presence of the poor, and they were for honor and splendor — which would not have been the case had he taken their livestock first." "<b>Further</b>, it appears that they intended to say that they do have silver, except that it is not available to them at present. And this is a common thing, that people do not always have silver on hand, for they send it to distant lands. And this is what the verse means by <b>אפס</b> — in the sense of withholding. Therefore they argued: why should they die, since their silver is in another place. And he answered them that they should give their livestock if the silver is withheld from them. And his intention is: if their words are true, that they do have silver but it is not available — and this indicates that he did not accept their claim, and he said to them that they should give him the livestock as a pledge for the silver until it arrives at a set time, and then he would return the livestock to them."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {0 "The phrase וַיִּשְׁבֹּר לָהֶם בְּכֶסֶף (rendered 'measured out to them in exchange for silver') alludes to Joseph's measuring/distributing grain, but here specifically by silver payment."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:41.236382942Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}